"Simply supported beam: moment diagram drawn by Python
'The <b>#python</b> block below runs first (numpy and matplotlib, in the browser). It draws the moment diagram and hands <i>L_m</i>, <i>w_kNm</i> and <i>M_max</i> back as Calcpad variables, which the lines after the block use. Python cannot read Calcpad variables yet, so the beam data lives in the block.
#python
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

L = 6.0        # span, m
w = 12.0       # uniform load, kN/m

x = np.linspace(0, L, 61)
M = w * x * (L - x) / 2        # bending moment, kN*m
M_max = float(M.max())
print("Span", L, "m, load", w, "kN/m")
print("Maximum moment", round(M_max, 2), "kN*m at x =", float(x[int(np.argmax(M))]), "m")

plt.figure(figsize=(7, 3.6))
plt.plot(x, M, label="M(x)")
plt.fill_between(x, 0, M, alpha=0.25)
plt.xlabel("x (m)")
plt.ylabel("M (kN*m)")
plt.title("Bending moment")
plt.grid(True)
plt.legend()
plt.show()

export(L_m=L, w_kNm=w, M_max=M_max)
#end python
"Design check with the exported values
E = 200GPa
I = 8*10^7mm^4
M_cap = 90kN*m
L = L_m*1m
w = w_kNm*1kN/m
M_Ed = M_max*1kN*m
η = M_Ed/M_cap
#if η ≤ 1
    'The moment is within the capacity.
#else
    'The moment exceeds the capacity.
#end if
δ_max = 5*w*L^4/(384*E*I)|mm
